Article 2

1. The Principles of Women's Empowerment are:a. respect for human rights;
b. gender equality and justice;
c. protection of victims' rights; andd. non-discriminatory.
2. The Objectives of Women's Empowerment and Protection are to:a. ensure the fulfillment of women's rights so that they can live and participate optimally in accordance with human dignity and dignity;
b. provide gender justice and equality; c. provide protection and security for women victims of violence and discrimination, as well as criminal acts of trafficking in persons;
d. eliminate all forms of violence and discrimination against women;
e. provide services to women Victims of violence, and witnesses; andf. carry out recovery of women Victims of violence to increase the participation of women both individually and in groups as potential and resources in the implementation of social community life.
3. The scope of this Regional Regulation includes: a. women's rights;
b. obligations and responsibilities of the regional government; c. women's empowerment;
d. women's protection;
e. protection of women victims of violence; f. prohibitions and sanctions;
g. community participation;
h. monitoring and evaluation; and i . funding.

Article 29

1. The City Government is obliged and responsible to carry out efforts to prevent violence against women in the form of: a. collecting data and information about victims of violence and laws and regulations;
b. conducting education on anti-violence values against women;
c. disseminating laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection for victims of violence;
d. monitoring and evaluating the implementation of protection for victims of violence; and e. preparing reports on the implementation of protection for women victims of violence.
2. To anticipate the occurrence of acts of violence against women, the City Government is obliged to provide and organize services for victims in the form of: a. establishing and facilitating the implementation of integrated service institutions for victims by involving community elements; and b. encouraging public awareness of the importance of protection for victims.
3. The City Government in carrying out its obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) must pay attention to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians or other persons who are legally responsible for the victim.

Article 17

The Mayor in implementing policies, programs and activities for women's protection undertakes efforts to: a. coordinate the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for women's protection among regional apparatus; b. cooperate with regencies/cities within one province and cooperation among regencies/cities in other provinces in the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for women's protection in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
c. strengthen institutional capacity for gender mainstreaming for the implementation of policies, programs and activities for women's protection;
d. facilitate women's protection services; e. provide women's protection services; f. implement affirmative action for women's protection; and g. prepare a data system for women's protection.

Article 33

To provide Protection as referred to in Article 27, the City Regional Government may undertake efforts to:
a. provide services and handling as quickly as possible to witnesses and/or victims;
b. provide ease, comfort, safety and free of charge for witnesses and/or victims;
c. maintain the confidentiality of witnesses and/or victims; d. guarantee justice and legal certainty for witnesses and/or victims;
e. provision of special service rooms at Police Offices;
f. provision of apparatus, health workers, social workers, and spiritual counselors;
g. creation and development of a system and mechanism for cooperation of service programs involving parties that are easily accessible to victims;
h. provide protection for companions, witnesses, family and friends of victims;
i. provide protection for female workers/laborers in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 9

The implementation of women's empowerment in the economic field as referred to in Article 8 letter a shall be carried out through:
a. provision of skills and job training; b. facilitation of the formation of productive economic business groups;
c. facilitation of strengthening and development of productive economic business groups;
d. facilitation and capital assistance; ande. facilitation of marketing network development.

CHAPTER VIII

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 36

1. The community may participate in the implementation of empowerment and protection of women.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by individuals, groups or social community organizations.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of:a. cooperation;
b. active role in policy formulation; and c. complaints/reports.
4. The obligations and responsibilities of the community in the protection of women are implemented through activities:a. preventing the occurrence of criminal acts;
b. providing protection to victims;
c. providing emergency assistance;
d. assisting in the process of submitting applications for protection orders; ande. providing information or reporting the occurrence of criminal acts of violence to law enforcement officials.
5. Further provisions regarding community participation as referred to in paragraph (3) are regulated in a Mayoral Regulation.

Article 27

1. The implementation of protection for women disaster victims in order to provide protection during disasters and post-disaster.
2. Women disaster victims as referred to in paragraph (1) shall receive protection over: a. access to decent housing;
b. health services;
c. access to information services and legal consultation; and
d. post-disaster recovery

Article 22

1. The implementation of protection for women with disabilities in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of discrimination includes, among others, freedom from cruel, inhuman treatment, degrading human dignity and freedom from exploitation.
2. Women with disabilities as referred to in paragraph (1) shall receive the right to protection over: a. guarantee of continuity and personal self-development;
b. participation in the fields of education, health, employment, politics, sports, arts and culture as well as the utilization of information and communication technology;
c. accessibility of information, legal aid, mobility, social services; and d. early intervention efforts including treatment and rehabilitation to improve their function and capacity.

Article 3

1. Every woman has the right to:a. live and maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and continue offspring;
c. develop her personality, to obtain education, and improve her quality of life to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process; f. be free from slavery or enslavement and threats;
g. obtain protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
h. obtain welfare and a decent life;
i. participate in politics;
j. perform legal acts;
k. freely choose a partner in her marriage; and l. receive fair wages in accordance with labor regulations.
2. The right to participate in politics as referred to in paragraph (1) letter i is, women have the right to:a. vote and/or be elected in general elections, regional head elections, or elections for political positions and other positions based on equal rights through direct, general, free, secret voting; andb. be appointed to every government position. (3) Every woman who is in a condition of disability, elderly, and/or pregnant, has the right to obtain facilities and special treatment.
4. Every elderly woman, person with physical disability and/or mental disability, has the right to obtain care, non-formal education.

Article 23

1. The implementation of protection for homeless women in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.
2. Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall receive the right to protection over: a. decent housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunities to obtain skills to improve economic status; and e. access to information and legal consultation services.

Article 24

1. The implementation of protection for domestic workers in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of discrimination, cruel, inhuman treatment, degrading human dignity and exploitation in employment relations.
2. Domestic workers as referred to in paragraph (1) shall receive protection over: a. recognition of rights, wages and decent working conditions;
b. health and social security of employment;
c. access to information and legal consultation services; and
d. opportunities to obtain knowledge and skills to improve economic status.

